"""`aspect setup bazelrc` — write a Bazel rc that configures vanilla `bazel` calls to
use Aspect's remote services and recommended optimizations. `aspect ci bazelrc`,
the name the CI integrations in the wild call, is the same task under its old
group.

`aspect <task>` injects its Bazel flags itself (`feature/workflows.axl`,
`feature/deployment.axl`, and `lib/aspect_flags.axl`, the table of every flag
Aspect sets and why). A bare `bazel build` started from the same job gets none
of that. This command writes the equivalent where vanilla `bazel` will find it.
It is the in-CLI successor to `rosetta bazelrc`: the CI integrations
(the setup-aspect GitHub Action, Buildkite plugin, CircleCI orb, GitLab
component) call it, as `aspect ci bazelrc`, once `rosetta` is gone from the
runner image.

Where the rc goes. One layout under one of two base directories: the generated
rc at `<base>/.aspect/bazelrc`, `try-import`ed from `<base>/.bazelrc`, the rc
Bazel already reads there, and the execution log under
`<base>/.aspect/generated/`. The import line is added once at the top of the
importing rc under a comment, a blank line apart from its other content — at the
top so that file's own lines come after and win where they disagree, which is
how a recommendation is overridden.

  - The checkout is the base off CI, and both files are meant to be
    committed, so everyone who builds the repository gets the same rc. The
    generated file is a starting point the repository then owns: edit it freely;
    a rerun of the command restores the generated version. Nothing in it may be
    this machine's: the credential helper is the tool's name, the CI host's
    group is left out, and the execution log is placed through `%workspace%`,
    the one variable Bazel expands in rc values, so the path holds in every
    clone. `.aspect/generated/` is where tools write into a checkout at build
    time; the command creates it with a `.gitignore` of `*` and `!.gitignore`,
    committed so the directory exists in every clone — Bazel does not create the
    log's parent. The workspace root is `ctx.std.env.bazel_root_dir()`, found by
    the boundary files Bazel finds `%workspace%` by, and the command refuses
    where none is.
  - The home directory is the base on CI, `--home`'s `auto`, and then the rc is
    this machine's: never committed, regenerated with `--force`, free to name
    the CI host's group and to hold absolute paths, and written without a
    workspace to write into. A CI checkout is a throwaway that some jobs commit
    from, so nothing of this machine's belongs in it; an ephemeral runner throws
    the home directory away with the job anyway. `~/.aspect/generated/` is
    created for the execution log, without the `.gitignore` a checkout gets.
    `--home=false` writes the checkout's rc on CI even so, for a job whose
    purpose is to regenerate the committed file.
  - An Aspect Workflows runner is always home mode. The runner is ours and
    nothing else configures Bazel on it, so rewriting the file every job is what
    keeps it in step with the runner's environment; tasks run with `--nohome_rc`
    and stream BES from the CLI, so nothing in it reaches them. Its execution
    log is the exception to the base: the job tmpdir, swept with the job, so
    concurrent jobs on a persistent runner do not share one. Both files land in
    a clean home: the Workflows agent removes `~/.aspect` outright and deletes
    `~/.bazelrc` before every job, so each job writes them from nothing and
    nothing carries over.

Whatever the importing rc already holds is kept, below the import, where
Bazel's last-wins gives it the final say over what it imports.

What a machine's rc turns on. Only a machine's rc enables anything; a checkout's
is shared by every host that builds the repository, so it stays inert until a
`--config` names something. A machine's enables the tuning that presumes no
endpoint — `aspect-common` and the CI host's group — and then whichever endpoints
`--remote` selects, so a plain `bazel build` on CI is cached and reports without
naming a `--config`.

`--remote` is the grammar `aspect build --remote` already uses: bare for the
cache and BES, `exec` to add remote execution, `no-cache` / `no-bes` / `no-exec`
to subtract, `none` for nothing. It defaults to `auto`: the cache and BES on CI —
a Workflows runner, the `ASPECT_WORKFLOWS_*` environment, or any CI host — and
nothing elsewhere, so a developer's `--home` rc does not quietly route their
local builds. Remote execution is never in `auto`, on a runner or off it: a
cluster may serve it while most jobs still want their actions local, so it waits
to be named. A capability the endpoints do not advertise cannot be turned on by
asking for it, and asking is said out loud: `--remote=exec` where the deployment
serves no executor — Aspect Cloud today — warns and leaves the rc as the rest of
the spec asked for.

One thing neither base solves: the execution log path is fixed per base, so two
concurrent builds of one checkout under different `--output_base`s overwrite
each other's log. Bazel expands only `%workspace%` in an rc value — there is no
output-base or invocation-id variable to key it by — so a repository that builds
that way drops the group (`--omit-bazel-flag=--execution_log_compact_file`).

What it holds: every flag sits in a named `--config` group from
`lib/rc_groups.axl`, and where the endpoints come from is what varies. Where the
environment names the deployment — the runner marker, or any `ASPECT_WORKFLOWS_*`
endpoint — the groups take their endpoints from it and no deployment sections are
written: a `--config=aspect-cloud` in a runner's rc would route a vanilla build
off the runner's own services. Where the environment is silent, a machine's rc
fills the same groups from the *default* deployment instead
(`deployment_environment`), which is what a `bazel` call on this machine would
reach anyway; `--remote` then selects among them exactly as it does on a runner.

Either way the configured deployments (`aspect auth status`) also get the opt-in
sections of `lib/deployment_rc.axl` — skipped where the environment already names
the endpoints — each chaining the common and CI-host groups and the tuning its
endpoints justify, so a call can name a deployment other than the default. A
checkout's rc has only those sections and enables none of them, which is what
makes it safe to commit and to import into a developer's own rc: the default
deployment is one machine's setting and never reaches it.

`--config=aspect-cloud` is always among the sections, and Aspect Cloud is the
default deployment until something else claims it: the built-in entry states its
own endpoints (`engine/aspect/auth.rs`), so the command works on a machine that
has never run `aspect auth login` and the rc it writes starts working the moment
there is a credential. `--announce_rc` names the group every flag came from.
Startup options have no `--config`, so the runner's output paths are plain
`startup` lines. The
credential helper the sections name is `aspect`, which Bazel resolves on PATH
like a shell would; a checkout's rc is shared by everyone who builds it, so it
names the tool, not a path, and the command warns when `aspect` is not on PATH
where it runs. A user who authenticates with a long-lived token instead drops
the helper in their own `~/.bazelrc` and sets `--remote_header`.

Bazel reads the home rc after the workspace rc, so on a runner a repository
whose own rc names a different `--bes_backend` for vanilla calls keeps it by
opting out: `--omit-bazel-flag=--bes_backend`. That knob drops any tuning flag
inside whichever group holds it (a group emptied that way disappears along with
the lines that named it); the endpoints, credentials, and runner paths in
`PROTECTED_FLAGS` are refused. Tasks have their own knob, `--bazel-flags:omit`
(`feature/bazel_flags.axl`), since vanilla `bazel` and tasks may want different
omissions.

The layout above is `rc_paths` in `lib/bazelrc_file.axl`, a pure function of the
base directory and this task's flags; how the rc renders and how the import line
is placed live there too. This file resolves the base — the only part that needs
the filesystem — and writes what comes back. Version-gated flags are resolved against the detected
Bazel version (assumed-latest when undetectable) before writing. Only file
output is supported — there is no stdout mode.
"""

load("./private/lib/aspect_endpoint_auth.axl", "can_authenticate")
load(
    "./private/lib/aspect_flags.axl",
    "omit_flags",
    "protected_omissions",
    "runner_exec_log_path",
    "runner_startup_flags",
    "unscoped",
)
load(
    "./private/lib/bazelrc_file.axl",
    "GENERATED_HEADER",
    "GENERATED_RC",
    "HOME_HEADER",
    "IMPORTING_RC",
    "ImportAction",
    "RUNNER_HEADER",
    "RcConfig",
    "command_sections",
    "defined_configs",
    "ensure_import",
    "import_line",
    "is_config_chain",
    "prune_configs",
    "rc_paths",
    "render_bazelrc",
    "startup_flags_for_rc",
)
load("./private/lib/deployment_flags.axl", "AUTO", "BARE", "REMOTE_CAPABILITIES", "capability_label", "join_and", "parse_remote")
load("./private/lib/deployment_rc.axl", "config_collisions", "config_name", "deployment_configs", "deployment_environment", "deployment_group_auth", "login_hint")
load("./private/lib/environment.axl", "bazel_misdetects_color", "detect_ci", "env_names_deployment", "get_workflows_environment", "info", "is_aspect_workflows_runner", "warn")
load("./private/lib/rc_groups.axl", "GROUP_COMMON", "HOST_GROUPS", "bes_chains", "enable_lines", "rc_groups", "section_chains")

_BAZEL_BOUNDARY_FILES = ["MODULE.bazel", "REPO.bazel", "WORKSPACE.bazel", "WORKSPACE"]

_HELPER_NAME = "aspect"

_IMPORT_VERBS = {
    ImportAction("created"): "created %s with it",
    ImportAction("unchanged"): "%s already imports it",
    ImportAction("prepended"): "added the import at the top of %s",
}

def _home(ctx: TaskContext) -> str:
    """The home directory home mode writes under; ends the task when it can't be
    resolved, since every path of that mode hangs off it."""
    home = ctx.std.env.home_dir()
    if home == None:
        ctx.std.process.exit(1, "Could not resolve your home directory, so there is nowhere to put ~/.aspect/bazelrc. Pass --output=<path> and --import-into=<rc> explicitly.")
    return home.rstrip("/")

def _find_workspace(ctx: TaskContext) -> str:
    """Bazel's workspace root — the directory it substitutes for `%workspace%`
    in an rc `import`, found from the working directory the way Bazel finds it,
    by the nearest boundary file. `""` where the working directory is in none,
    which home mode does not need."""
    root = ctx.std.env.bazel_root_dir().rstrip("/")
    if not any([ctx.std.fs.exists(root + "/" + marker) for marker in _BAZEL_BOUNDARY_FILES]):
        return ""
    return root

def _workspace(ctx: TaskContext) -> str:
    """`_find_workspace`, ending the task where there is none: with no workspace
    there is no `.bazelrc` for vanilla `bazel` to read."""
    root = _find_workspace(ctx)
    if not root:
        ctx.std.process.exit(1, "No Bazel workspace above %s (no MODULE.bazel, REPO.bazel, or WORKSPACE), so there is no .bazelrc for vanilla bazel to read. Run this from inside the workspace, or pass --output=<path> and --import-into=<rc>." % ctx.std.env.current_dir())
    return root

def _repo_defined_configs(ctx: TaskContext) -> list:
    """The `--config` sections this checkout already defines for itself, or `[]`
    where it defines none.

    A checkout that carries its own generated rc — its `.bazelrc` imports
    `%workspace%/.aspect/bazelrc` and that file is there — has already said what
    `bazel` can reach here, in a file the repository committed and is free to have
    edited. Both halves are required: a `try-import` whose file is missing
    configures nothing.

    What comes back is read from that file rather than assumed. A machine's rc
    that merely *enables* one of these sections adds nothing to override, which
    is how the two rcs stay out of each other's way — but Bazel fails outright on
    a `--config` no rc defines, so enabling a section has to be founded on
    knowing it is there."""
    workspace = _find_workspace(ctx)
    if not workspace:
        return []
    importer = workspace + "/" + IMPORTING_RC
    if not ctx.std.fs.exists(workspace + "/" + GENERATED_RC) or not ctx.std.fs.exists(importer):
        return []
    if import_line("%workspace%/" + GENERATED_RC) not in ctx.std.fs.read_to_string(importer).split("\n"):
        return []
    return defined_configs(ctx.std.fs.read_to_string(workspace + "/" + GENERATED_RC))

def _create_generated_dir(ctx: TaskContext, dir: str, gitignore: bool) -> list:
    """Create `dir`, the execution log's parent, which Bazel will not create
    itself. A checkout's also gets a `.gitignore` of everything but itself:
    committed, that makes the directory exist in every clone while nothing
    written there is ever tracked. A home directory is not a checkout, so there
    it is the bare directory. Returns the files written, for the report."""
    ctx.std.fs.create_dir_all(dir)
    if not gitignore:
        return []
    ctx.std.fs.write(dir + "/.gitignore", "*\n!.gitignore\n")
    return [dir + "/.gitignore"]

def _write(ctx: TaskContext, path: str, content: str) -> None:
    """Write `content` to `path`, creating parent directories."""
    parent = path.rstrip("/").rsplit("/", 1)
    if len(parent) == 2 and parent[0] and not ctx.std.fs.exists(parent[0]):
        ctx.std.fs.create_dir_all(parent[0])
    ctx.std.fs.write(path, content)

def _resolve(ctx: TaskContext, flags: list, bazel_version: str | None) -> list:
    """Resolve the version-gated `(flag, constraint)` tuples in `flags` against the
    detected Bazel version via the bazelrc runtime: a blank RunCommand whose
    `common` bucket holds every flag, expanded assumed-latest when the version is
    unknown. Config chain entries are kept verbatim ahead of the rest — the blank
    RunCommand has no section for them to expand into. Command scopes are
    dropped: the rc writes every flag under `common`, which Bazel applies only
    where the option is accepted."""
    chain = [f for f in flags if is_config_chain(f)]
    rest = [unscoped(f) for f in flags if not is_config_chain(f)]
    rc = ctx.bazel.new_rc(flags = rest, version = bazel_version)
    return chain + rc.expand(command = "common")

def _home_mode(value: str, on_ci: bool) -> bool:
    """Whether to write the machine's rc rather than the checkout's.

    `auto` — the default — is CI, where the checkout is a throwaway a job may
    well commit from and the home directory is the safe place for a machine's
    own configuration. Off CI it is the checkout, which is what someone setting
    a repository up wants. `--home=false` forces that even on CI."""
    if value == AUTO or not value:
        return on_ci
    if value not in ["true", "false"]:
        fail("--home: expected true, false, or auto; got '%s'." % value)
    return value == "true"

def _remote_spec(value: str, on_ci: bool):
    """The `--remote` capability set this rc enables, with `auto` resolved.

    `auto` — the default — is the accelerators that cannot change build semantics
    on CI, where a `bazel` call has no one to ask, and nothing on a developer's
    machine, where the rc stays inert until a `--config` names something. Every
    other value is the same grammar as `aspect build --remote`, down to the
    `no-` forms and `none`."""
    tokens = [t.strip() for t in value.split(",")]
    if AUTO in tokens:
        if len(tokens) > 1:
            fail("--remote: 'auto' cannot be combined with other capabilities; got '%s'." % value)
        return parse_remote(BARE if on_ci else "none")
    return parse_remote(value or (BARE if on_ci else "none"))

def _ci_reason(on_runner: bool, env_deployment: bool, ci_host) -> str:
    """Why the `auto` defaults believe this is CI, or `""` when they do not.
    Named so the rc's two decisions can attribute themselves to the same
    evidence — the runner, the environment naming a deployment, or the CI host
    `detect_ci` recognized."""
    if on_runner:
        return "this is an Aspect Workflows runner"
    if env_deployment:
        return "the ASPECT_WORKFLOWS_* environment names a deployment"
    if ci_host != None:
        return "this is CI (%s)" % ci_host
    return ""

def _announce_home_mode(ctx: TaskContext, value: str, home_mode: bool, reason: str):
    """Say which rc `--home=auto` chose and on what evidence, so a file written
    outside the checkout — or inside it — is never a surprise."""
    if value not in ["", AUTO]:
        return
    if home_mode:
        info(ctx.std, "--home=auto: %s, so this machine's rc under ~/.aspect is written rather than the checkout's. `--home=false` writes the checkout's pair instead." % reason)
    else:
        info(ctx.std, "--home=auto: not CI, so the checkout's %s pair is written rather than this machine's. `--home=true` writes ~/.aspect/bazelrc instead." % GENERATED_RC)

def _announce_remote_spec(ctx: TaskContext, value: str, spec, reason: str, machine_rc: bool):
    """Say what `--remote=auto` turned on and on what evidence. The endpoints are
    defined either way; this is about what the rc enables without a `--config`,
    which only a machine's rc does."""
    if value not in ["", AUTO]:
        return
    if not machine_rc:
        info(ctx.std, "--remote=auto: a checkout's rc enables nothing — it is shared by every host that builds here, so its sections stay behind a --config. `--home=true` writes this machine's rc, where --remote decides what is on.")
        return
    wanted = [capability_label(name) for name in REMOTE_CAPABILITIES if spec.on[name]]
    if wanted:
        info(ctx.std, "--remote=auto: %s, so the deployment's %s %s enabled for every `bazel` build here. `--remote=none` leaves them defined but off." %
                      (reason, join_and(wanted), "is" if len(wanted) == 1 else "are"))
    elif not reason:
        # On CI with nothing enabled, the decline said why in its own words.
        info(ctx.std, "--remote=auto: not CI, so no endpoint is enabled — a developer's rc stays inert until a --config names one. `--remote` enables the remote cache and BES backend here.")

def _sections_to_enable(default, spec) -> list:
    """The deployment sections a machine's rc would turn on for `spec`: the
    deployment's own where the cache or BES is wanted, and its `-exec` companion
    where remote execution is. Empty when `--remote` enables nothing.

    A deployment that serves no executor has no `-exec` section for anything to
    define, so it is not asked for: a checkout's rc would be reported as missing
    a section neither it nor this command ever writes."""
    wanted = []
    if spec.on["cache"] or spec.on["bes"]:
        wanted.append(config_name(default))
    if spec.on["exec"] and _serves_remote_execution(None, default):
        wanted.append(config_name(default) + "-exec")
    return wanted

def _serves_remote_execution(endpoint_env, default) -> bool:
    """Whether anything here can route actions to an executor: the environment's
    own (a runner's `ASPECT_WORKFLOWS_REMOTE_EXECUTOR`), or the default
    deployment's `exec` endpoint. `endpoint_env` may be `None` to ask about the
    deployment alone."""
    if endpoint_env != None and endpoint_env.remote_executor != None:
        return True
    return default != None and default.exec != ""

def _default_deployment(deployments: list):
    """The deployment a `bazel` call reaches without naming one, or `None`."""
    for d in deployments:
        if d.default:
            return d
    return None

def _groups(ctx: TaskContext, environment, on_runner: bool, machine_rc: bool, remote_on: dict, auth: dict, exec_log_path: str) -> (list, list):
    """`rc_groups` for this host: every group defined, and the names enabled."""
    return rc_groups(environment, on_runner, bazel_misdetects_color(ctx.std.env), exec_log_path, machine_rc, remote_on, auth)

def _credential_helper(ctx: TaskContext) -> str:
    """What the sections hand Bazel as the credential helper: `aspect`, which
    Bazel resolves on PATH the way a shell does. A checkout's rc is read by
    everyone who builds it, so it names the tool, never this machine's path to
    it; where `aspect` is missing from PATH here, say so, since a vanilla build
    on this machine would fail at the helper."""
    if ctx.std.process.which(_HELPER_NAME) == None:
        warn(ctx.std, "`aspect` is not on PATH here. The rc names it as Bazel's credential helper, so put it on PATH before a vanilla `bazel` reads the rc (the CI integrations install it).")
    return _HELPER_NAME

def _sections(deployments: list, helper: str, groups: list) -> list:
    """The `--config` sections for this machine's configured deployments, each
    chaining `section_chains(groups)` first and, with a BES, `bes_chains(groups)`."""
    return deployment_configs(deployments, helper, section_chains(groups), bes_chains(groups))

def _bazelrc_impl(ctx: TaskContext) -> int | TaskConclusion:
    omitted = ctx.args.omit_bazel_flags
    blocked = protected_omissions(omitted)
    if blocked:
        return TaskConclusion(exit_code = 1, message = "--omit-bazel-flag cannot drop %s: endpoints, credentials, and the runner's paths are what make the build Aspect's." % ", ".join(blocked))

    if ctx.args.home == "true" and ctx.args.output:
        return TaskConclusion(exit_code = 1, message = "--home and --output both choose where the rc goes. Pass one: --home writes ~/.aspect/bazelrc, --output writes the path you name.")

    environment = get_workflows_environment(ctx.std)
    on_runner = is_aspect_workflows_runner(ctx.std.env)

    # What both `auto` defaults read. `detect_ci`, not `environment.ci`, which
    # names only the four hosts with their own branches — a generic `CI` is still
    # CI — and the env naming the endpoints is the same answer by another means.
    env_deployment = env_names_deployment(ctx.std.env)
    on_ci = on_runner or env_deployment or detect_ci(ctx.std.env) != None
    home_mode = _home_mode(ctx.args.home, on_ci)
    ci_reason = _ci_reason(on_runner, env_deployment, detect_ci(ctx.std.env))
    _announce_home_mode(ctx, ctx.args.home, home_mode, ci_reason)

    startup_flags = []
    runner_log = ""
    if on_runner:
        if environment.runner == None:
            return TaskConclusion(exit_code = 1, message = "Running on an Aspect Workflows runner but the runner metadata env vars are missing, so the cache flags cannot be generated. This usually means an older runner image; upgrade the runner.")
        startup_flags = startup_flags_for_rc(runner_startup_flags(environment, ctx.std.env.aspect_root_dir()))

        # Per job and swept with it, so concurrent jobs do not share one log.
        runner_log = runner_exec_log_path(environment)

    # `--output` and `--import-into` together name every path `rc_paths` would
    # otherwise derive, so neither base has to exist to honor them.
    overridden = ctx.args.output and ctx.args.import_into
    base = "" if overridden else (_home(ctx) if home_mode else _workspace(ctx))
    paths = rc_paths(base, output = ctx.args.output, import_into = ctx.args.import_into, home_mode = home_mode, exec_log = runner_log)

    # This machine's when it is written outside the checkout, and also when the
    # env names the endpoints, which are this machine's wherever the file lands.
    machine_rc = home_mode or env_deployment
    spec = _remote_spec(ctx.args.remote, on_ci)

    # Where the env names the endpoints they are already in hand; otherwise a
    # machine's rc takes them from the default deployment, which is what a
    # `bazel` call on this machine would reach. A checkout's rc takes neither:
    # both are this machine's, and it is shared by every host that builds here.
    deployments = [] if env_deployment else ctx.aspect.auth.deployments()
    helper = _HELPER_NAME if env_deployment else _credential_helper(ctx)
    default = _default_deployment(deployments) if machine_rc else None

    # A checkout that defines its own sections is enabled rather than restated,
    # so nothing here can override what the repository edited. Not where the
    # endpoints are this machine's: a runner's services are nowhere in a
    # checkout, and are meant to win.
    repo_defines = [] if on_runner or env_deployment or ctx.args.output else _repo_defined_configs(ctx)
    chain_to_repo = []
    if home_mode and default != None and repo_defines:
        wanted = _sections_to_enable(default, spec)
        if GROUP_COMMON in repo_defines and all([w in repo_defines for w in wanted]):
            chain_to_repo = [GROUP_COMMON] + wanted
        elif wanted:
            info(ctx.std, "This checkout's %s does not define %s, so this machine's rc carries its own copy of those endpoints. `aspect setup bazelrc --force` in the checkout regenerates it." %
                          ("%workspace%/" + GENERATED_RC, ", ".join([w for w in wanted if w not in repo_defines])))

    # Nothing to fill where the checkout's own sections carry the endpoints and
    # this rc only names them.
    endpoint_env = environment
    auth = {}
    if default != None and not chain_to_repo:
        # `auto` declines a deployment nothing here can authenticate, since the
        # helper reaching its endpoints would have no token to give and Bazel
        # treats that as fatal. An explicit `--remote` is the caller's to make.
        if ctx.args.remote in ["", AUTO] and not can_authenticate(ctx, default):
            spec = parse_remote("none")
            info(ctx.std, "Not enabling %s's remote cache or BES: nothing here can authenticate it (%s). The rc still defines --config=%s, which works once there is a credential." %
                          (default.name, login_hint(default), config_name(default)))
        endpoint_env = deployment_environment(default, environment.ci)
        auth = deployment_group_auth(default, helper)

    # Asked for an executor where there is none. Nothing downstream fails over
    # it — no group is enabled and no section names one — so the rc would come
    # out as if the flag had not been passed.
    if machine_rc and spec.on["exec"] and not _serves_remote_execution(endpoint_env, default):
        warn(ctx.std, "Not enabling remote execution: %s. `--remote=exec` adds nothing to this rc; the remote cache and BES are unaffected." %
                      ("%s advertises no remote executor" % default.name if default != None else "nothing here advertises a remote executor"))

    _announce_remote_spec(ctx, ctx.args.remote, spec, ci_reason, machine_rc)

    (groups, enabled) = _groups(ctx, endpoint_env, on_runner, machine_rc, spec.on, auth, paths.exec_log)
    if chain_to_repo:
        # `aspect-common` and the endpoint groups are the checkout's; keep only
        # the host group, which a committed rc never holds, and enable what the
        # checkout defines alongside it.
        groups = [g for g in groups if g.name in HOST_GROUPS.values()]
        enabled = [g.name for g in groups] + chain_to_repo
    sections = [] if env_deployment or chain_to_repo else _sections(deployments, helper, groups)

    # A backstop: the built-in Aspect Cloud entry states its own endpoints, so
    # there is always at least one section. Without it an rc naming no endpoint
    # would be a silent no-op, and the report would take `max()` of nothing.
    if not env_deployment and not chain_to_repo and not sections:
        return TaskConclusion(exit_code = 1, message = "No deployment here advertises an endpoint for the rc to point `bazel` at. Run `aspect auth configure <host>` for the deployment you build against, then rerun `aspect setup bazelrc`.")
    configs = groups + sections
    collisions = config_collisions(configs)
    if collisions:
        return TaskConclusion(exit_code = 1, message = "\n".join(collisions))

    bazel_version = ctx.bazel.version()
    startup_flags = omit_flags(startup_flags, omitted)
    configs = [
        RcConfig(name = c.name, source = c.source, comment = c.comment, flags = _resolve(ctx, omit_flags(c.flags, omitted), bazel_version))
        for c in configs
    ]
    (configs, enabled) = prune_configs(configs, enabled)

    written = []
    if paths.generated_dir:
        written.extend(_create_generated_dir(ctx, paths.generated_dir, gitignore = not home_mode))

    # Left as it is unless `--force`, which the file's own first line says. A
    # Workflows runner is unaffected: its agent removes `~/.aspect` before every
    # job, so there is never a file here to keep.
    kept = not ctx.args.force and ctx.std.fs.exists(paths.output)
    if not kept:
        header = RUNNER_HEADER if on_runner else (HOME_HEADER if home_mode else GENERATED_HEADER)
        sections = command_sections(bazel_version)
        _write(ctx, paths.output, render_bazelrc(startup_flags, enable_lines(enabled, sections), sections, configs, header = header))
    written.insert(0, paths.output)
    verb = "Kept" if kept else "Wrote"

    import_result = None
    if paths.importer:
        existing = ctx.std.fs.read_to_string(paths.importer) if ctx.std.fs.exists(paths.importer) else None
        import_result = ensure_import(existing, import_line(paths.imported))
        if import_result.action != ImportAction("unchanged"):
            _write(ctx, paths.importer, import_result.content)

    shown = lambda path: _relative(path, base, home_mode)
    if chain_to_repo:
        info(ctx.std, "%s %s: enabled %s. The endpoints are this checkout's own %s, which this rc only turns on — nothing here restates them." %
                      (verb, shown(paths.output), ", ".join(enabled), "%workspace%/" + GENERATED_RC))
        return 0
    if env_deployment:
        optional = [c.name for c in configs if c.name not in enabled]
        summary = "%s %s: enabled --config groups %s; opt-in %s (the ASPECT_WORKFLOWS_* environment names this machine's deployment, so no deployment sections)" % (verb, shown(paths.output), ", ".join(enabled), ", ".join(optional) or "none")
        if import_result != None:
            summary += "; " + _IMPORT_VERBS[import_result.action] % shown(paths.importer)
        info(ctx.std, summary + ".")
        return 0

    group_names = [g.name for g in groups]
    resolved_sections = [c for c in configs if c.name not in group_names]
    info(ctx.std, "Generating %s: Bazel flags that point plain `bazel` calls at Aspect Cloud and, when configured, Aspect single-tenant deployments." % shown(paths.output))
    for line in _report(written, import_result, paths, shown, resolved_sections, enabled, home_mode, verb):
        print(line)
    return 0

def _relative(path: str, base: str, home_mode: bool) -> str:
    """`path` as the summary shows it: relative to `base` when under it —
    `~/`-prefixed in home mode, where a bare `.aspect/bazelrc` would read as the
    checkout's — and absolute otherwise."""
    prefix = base + "/"
    if not base or not path.startswith(prefix):
        return path
    return ("~/" if home_mode else "") + path[len(prefix):]

def _report(written: list, import_result, paths, shown, sections: list, enabled: list, home_mode: bool, verb: str) -> list:
    """What the command did and how to use the result, printed after the lead
    line: the files written, whether the import was added or already there, and
    whether they belong to the repository or to this machine, then any groups
    already enabled, then the `--config` names the rc defines and what each
    wires, then that Aspect's own tasks reach the default deployment with
    `--remote` and need none of it, last so it is what a developer sees."""
    lines = [""]
    for i, path in enumerate(written):
        # Padded by hand: AXL's `%` formatting rejects width flags.
        label = (verb if i == 0 else "Wrote").lower()
        lines.append("  " + label + " " * (6 - len(label)) + shown(path))
    if import_result != None:
        if import_result.action == ImportAction("unchanged"):
            lines.append("  kept   %s (already imports %s)" % (shown(paths.importer), paths.imported))
        else:
            lines.append("  added  try-import %s at the top of %s" % (paths.imported, shown(paths.importer)))
    lines.append("These are this machine's, outside the checkout." if home_mode else "Commit these with your repository.")

    if enabled:
        lines.append("")
        lines.append("Applied to every bazel call on this machine: %s." % ", ".join(enabled))

    lines.append("")
    lines.append("Choose a deployment with --config on any bazel command:")
    lines.append("")
    width = max([len(c.name) for c in sections]) + len("--config=")
    for c in sections:
        flag = "--config=" + c.name
        lines.append("  %s%s  %s" % (flag, " " * (width - len(flag)), c.comment))
    lines.append("")
    lines.append("An existing file is left as it is; `aspect setup bazelrc --force` regenerates it." if home_mode else "The file is a starting point: edit it as your repository needs. `aspect setup bazelrc --force` regenerates it, discarding those edits.")

    lines.append("")
    lines.append("`aspect <task>` needs no --config and no rc: it reaches the default deployment itself, on CI without a flag and elsewhere with --remote (`aspect build --remote`).")
    return lines

_BAZELRC_ARGS = {
    "home": args.string(
        long = "home",
        default = AUTO,
        bare = "true",
        description = "Write the machine's rc under the home directory — `~/.aspect/bazelrc`, imported by `~/.bazelrc` — instead of the checkout's `.aspect/bazelrc`, which is meant to be committed. `auto`, the default, is on for CI (an Aspect Workflows runner, the ASPECT_WORKFLOWS_* environment, or any CI host) and off elsewhere: a CI checkout is a throwaway a job may commit from, the home directory is where a machine's own configuration belongs, and only a machine's rc enables anything. Pass --home=false to write the checkout's rc anyway. Cannot be combined with --output, which names the path itself.",
    ),
    "output": args.string(
        default = "",
        description = "Rc file to write instead of the default `<base>/.aspect/bazelrc` — the base being the home directory on CI and the workspace root elsewhere, per --home. Parent directories are created if needed. Only file output is supported — there is no stdout mode.",
    ),
    "import_into": args.string(
        long = "import-into",
        default = "",
        description = "Rc that receives the `try-import` line for the written rc, added at its top only when not already present, so the file's own lines override the import. Defaults to the `.bazelrc` of whichever base the rc was written under: the workspace one, importing `%workspace%/.aspect/bazelrc`, or `~/.bazelrc` on CI, importing the written path.",
    ),
    "remote": args.string(
        long = "remote",
        default = AUTO,
        bare = BARE,
        description = "Which of the deployment's remote endpoints this rc turns on for every `bazel` build, test or run, in the same grammar as `aspect build --remote`: bare --remote enables its remote cache and BES backend, `exec` adds remote execution (never implied), `no-cache` / `no-bes` / `no-exec` subtract, and `none` enables nothing. The default, `auto`, is bare --remote on CI — where a `bazel` call has no one to ask — and `none` elsewhere, leaving a developer's rc inert until a --config names something. Endpoints come from the ASPECT_WORKFLOWS_* environment where it names them, and from the default deployment otherwise. Only a machine's rc enables anything: a checkout's is shared by every host that builds it, so its sections stay opt-in whatever this says.",
    ),
    "force": args.boolean(
        default = False,
        description = "Regenerate the rc even though it is already there, discarding whatever was edited into it. Without this an existing one is left alone: a checkout's is a starting point the repository owns, and a machine's may belong to a persistent runner someone set up by hand. An Aspect Workflows runner reaches this fresh every job — its agent removes ~/.aspect beforehand — so the two behave the same there.",
    ),
    "omit_bazel_flags": args.string_list(
        long = "omit-bazel-flag",
        description = "A Bazel flag this rc would otherwise set that you do not want, named as Aspect spells it (e.g. --omit-bazel-flag=--heap_dump_on_oom; a value after `=` is ignored). Repeat for several. Set it once per repo in config.axl via ctx.tasks[\"setup/bazelrc\"].args.omit_bazel_flags. Endpoints, credentials, and the runner's output paths cannot be omitted. Tasks have their own knob, --bazel-flags:omit.",
    ),
}

bazelrc = task(
    group = ["setup"],
    summary = "Write a Bazel rc that points vanilla `bazel` calls at Aspect's remote services and recommended optimizations.",
    description = "Write a Bazel rc so vanilla `bazel` calls pick up the same configuration that `aspect <task>` injects. Off CI the rc goes to `<workspace>/.aspect/bazelrc` and the workspace `.bazelrc` gains `try-import %workspace%/.aspect/bazelrc` at its top under a comment, added once, so the repository's own lines override — commit both. On CI the same pair is written under the home directory instead (`~/.aspect/bazelrc`, imported by `~/.bazelrc`): the machine's own rc, never committed, so nothing of this machine's lands in a checkout a job may commit from. That is --home's `auto`; --home=false writes the checkout's rc on CI even so. Every flag sits in a named `--config` group — `aspect-common`, `aspect-cache`, `aspect-bes`, `aspect-<ci host>`, `aspect-runner`, and the opt-in `aspect-exec` — and a machine's rc enables at the top the groups that apply on this host (`bazel build --announce_rc` shows which group set what). On CI that is the recommended tuning plus a remote cache and BES — the runner's own where the `ASPECT_WORKFLOWS_*` environment names them, the default deployment's otherwise — while remote execution waits to be named, since a cluster may serve it where most jobs still want their actions local. --remote picks among them in the same grammar as `aspect build --remote` (bare, `exec`, `no-cache` / `no-bes` / `no-exec`, `none`); its default, `auto`, is that CI behavior and nothing off CI. Deployment sections let a call name a deployment other than the default, each bringing the common and CI-host tuning with it: `--config=aspect-cloud` for Aspect Cloud's remote cache + BES, `--config=aspect-<name>` for a configured deployment (`aspect auth status`), and `--config=aspect-<name>-exec` to add its remote execution — authenticated through the `aspect get` credential helper. No section names the default deployment, which `aspect auth use` could change under a committed file. `aspect ci bazelrc` is an alias.",
    implementation = _bazelrc_impl,
    args = _BAZELRC_ARGS,
)

ci_bazelrc = task(
    group = ["ci"],
    kind = "bazelrc",
    summary = "Alias of `aspect setup bazelrc`, kept for the CI integrations that call it.",
    description = "The same command as `aspect setup bazelrc`, under the name the setup-aspect GitHub Action, Buildkite plugin, CircleCI orb, and GitLab component call; new callers should use `aspect setup bazelrc`. Config overrides for this name go to ctx.tasks[\"ci/bazelrc\"].",
    implementation = _bazelrc_impl,
    args = _BAZELRC_ARGS,
)
